id: rust.async-streams
kind: rust
title: Async streams
aliases: stream, StreamExt, async iterator
tags: async, streams
questions:
  - how do you process a stream of items asynchronously in Rust?
invariant: A Stream yields items over time, pulled by the consumer, so backpressure comes from the consumer not asking for the next item.
answer_30s: Streams are async iterators with pull-based backpressure, and their value is composition: bounded concurrency, early termination and error handling per item.
when_to_use:
  - pipelines over paged APIs, large files, or queue consumers with bounded in-flight work
avoid_when:
  - a single value (a future) or a collection already fully in memory
minimal_example: |
  use futures::stream::{self, StreamExt};

  let mut s = stream::iter(1..=100).map(|n| async move { n * 2 }).buffered(4);
  while let Some(v) = s.next().await {
      tracing::debug!(v, "processed");
  }
failure_modes:
  - collecting a large stream into a Vec, losing the memory bound
  - dropping a stream mid-pipeline and assuming the producer stopped cleanly
related:
  - rust.bounded-concurrency
  - rust.tokio-mpsc
  - design.file-pipeline
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/futures/latest/futures/stream/trait.Stream.html
